--TEST--
An uncaught throw runs `Core\Fatal::onUncaughtThrow` with the live `Throwable` and then the exit queue, and neither hook changes the ending the floor still reports at status 1 — `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw`, `rule:errors/engine-floor`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/an-uncaught-throw
// `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw` puts two observers on this ending and gives
// each a different view of it: the handler sees the `Throwable` itself, the
// exit queue behind it sees the report. The case asserts the order of the two
// and that the record below is written regardless, which is the half that
// makes "there is no `set_exception_handler`" a fact rather than an absence.

Core\Fatal::onUncaughtThrow(fn (Throwable $failure): void => {
    echo "handler: ", $failure->message, "\n";
});

// Not a tier of the ladder: it runs at every non-fatal ending, and this case
// pins what it reads off the one an uncaught throw causes.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    echo "exit hook: status ", $report->status(), "\n";
    echo "exit hook: uncaught ",
        $report->reason() == Core\Script\ExitReason::UncaughtThrow ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
    echo "exit hook: error ", $report->error()?->message ?? "none", "\n";
});

echo "working\n";

// Thrown at the file's top level, so the backtrace is the root frame alone.
throw new RuntimeError("nothing catches this");
--EXPECT--
working
handler: nothing catches this
exit hook: status 1
exit hook: uncaught yes
exit hook: error nothing catches this
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
{"level":"error","msg":"nothing catches this","fields":{"class":"RuntimeError"},"nodes":[{"function":"<script>","file":"%s","line":%d}]}
